const canonical = "https://samedayappliance.repair/services/refrigerator-repair/kitchenaid-not-cooling/";

const title = "KitchenAid Refrigerator Not Cooling? Fix & Causes";
const description = "KitchenAid refrigerator not cooling? It's premium Whirlpool inside — dirty coils, the start relay, the evaporator fan, or defrost. Built-in models need the coils and airflow kept clear. Safe checks, food-safety timing, when to call.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my KitchenAid refrigerator not cooling?",
    a: "KitchenAid is Whirlpool's premium line and its refrigerators run on the Whirlpool platform, so the causes are the familiar short list: dirty condenser coils, a failed compressor start relay (often 'clicking' at the back), a failed evaporator fan ('freezer cold, fridge warm'), a defrost system iced over the evaporator, the control board, and — the serious end — a locked compressor or sealed-system fault. On KitchenAid's built-in and counter-depth models, restricted airflow around the coils is an especially common trigger because these units are tucked tight into cabinetry. Cleaning the coils and clearing airflow are yours to do; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job."
  },
  {
    q: "My KitchenAid is built-in — does that change the diagnosis?",
    a: "The parts are the same Whirlpool-platform components, but built-in and counter-depth KitchenAid units run hotter in their cabinet cutouts, so airflow faults show up more. The condenser needs room to shed heat; when a built-in's grille is blocked or the coils are dusty, it loses cooling faster than a freestanding fridge would. On built-ins the condenser is often reached through a top or toe-kick grille rather than the back, and many carry an internal condenser fan that a tech checks along with the coils. Keep the grille clear, and if cleaning it doesn't restore cooling, it's a service call — the sealed system on a built-in is not a DIY area."
  },
  {
    q: "My KitchenAid freezer is cold but the fridge is warm — what's wrong?",
    a: "That split — cold freezer, warm fridge — is the classic evaporator fan failure, same as on any Whirlpool-platform fridge. The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge; when it fails or frost from a defrost fault jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. On KitchenAid the frost usually traces back to a defrost heater, sensor, or control that's stopped running its cycle. Both are fixable, tech-level repairs inside the cabinet. Keep the doors closed while you wait — the fridge food is on roughly a four-hour clock."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the clicking sound from the back of my KitchenAid?",
    a: "A click every few minutes from the bottom-rear, with the fridge slowly warming, is often the compressor start relay cycling — it tries to start the compressor, fails, trips its overload, and tries again. On the Whirlpool platform KitchenAid uses, that relay is a common and well-understood no-cool part. Don't keep running it in that state; the repeated starting stresses the compressor. This is a tech diagnosis and repair. On a built-in KitchenAid especially, get it looked at promptly — these are expensive units and a stressed compressor is the costly end of the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "How long is food safe in a KitchenAid that stopped cooling?",
    a: "Per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door kept closed, and a full freezer holds a safe temperature for roughly 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if you keep it shut. Perishable food — meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, leftovers — held above 40°F for 2 hours or more should be thrown out. When in doubt, throw it out, and never taste food to check. That 4-hour fridge window is why a no-cool refrigerator is a same-day call — keep the doors closed and get a tech out before it closes."
  }
];

