/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-cool KitchenAid is Whirlpool-platform parts — with a built-in twist.</h2>

    <p><strong>KitchenAid is Whirlpool's premium line, built on the Whirlpool platform, so a running-but-warm KitchenAid comes down to the same short list: dirty condenser coils, a failed compressor start relay (often "clicking" at the back), a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm"), a defrost system iced over, the control board, and the serious end — a locked compressor or sealed-system fault. The KitchenAid twist is airflow: its built-in and counter-depth models sit tight in cabinetry and run hot, so blocked grilles and dusty coils cost cooling faster than on a freestanding fridge. Clear the grille, clean the coils, check airflow first. Those are yours to do; the relay, board, and sealed system are a tech job. A no-cool fridge is a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 BUILT-IN DIFFERENTIATOR -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Built-in and counter-depth run hot</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">On a KitchenAid, airflow is the first suspect.</h2>

    <p>KitchenAid's built-in and counter-depth refrigerators are designed to sit flush in cabinetry, which looks great and traps heat. The condenser still has to shed the compressor's heat somewhere, and on these models it usually vents through a top or toe-kick grille rather than out the back. When that grille is blocked or the coils behind it are packed with dust, the fridge loses cooling — and it happens faster than on a freestanding unit because there's less room for the heat to escape.</p>

    <p>Many built-ins also carry an internal condenser fan that pulls air across the coils; a tech checks that fan along with the coils when airflow is the suspect. So on a KitchenAid the very first move is to clear the grille and vacuum the coils. If that restores cooling, you're done. If it doesn't, the fault is deeper — and the sealed system on a premium built-in is emphatically not a DIY area. This is the same platform we cover on the <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool not cooling</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a KitchenAid stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Blocked airflow / dirty coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>On built-in and counter-depth KitchenAids especially, a blocked grille or dust-caked coils stop the condenser shedding heat, and cooling drops. The most common and most homeowner-fixable cause.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor start relay — the "clicking"</h3>
      <p>A click every few minutes from the bottom-rear with the fridge warming is often the start relay failing to start the compressor. A common, well-understood Whirlpool-platform no-cool part — but a tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan blows cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails or frost jams it, the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor runs but doesn't cool or won't start, the sealed system is the fault — a gauges-and-tech diagnosis, and the higher-ticket end. On a premium built-in, worth diagnosing before it's left running warm.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Clear the grille and vacuum the coils</strong> — on a built-in, check the top or toe-kick grille, not just the back. Blocked airflow is the #1 KitchenAid cause.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Power-cycle a freestanding unit</strong> — pull it off the wall, unplug a minute, restart. (Leave a hardwired built-in to the tech.)</li>
      <li><strong>Note the symptoms</strong> — freezer cold while fridge is warm, frost on the back panel, or clicking at the rear — so we can pinpoint the fault faster.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> grille, coils, vents, seal, and temperature are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor or start relay, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool KitchenAid is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Grille clear, coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the grille is clear, the coils are clean, and the KitchenAid still won't cool, the fault is inside — the start relay, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the compressor and sealed system. Because KitchenAid shares the Whirlpool platform, our techs carry the common start relays, fan motors, and defrost parts, and we're comfortable working the built-in and counter-depth cabinets these premium units come in.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide, the shared-platform <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/whirlpool-not-cooling/">Whirlpool not cooling</a> page, and the sibling brands <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/maytag-not-cooling/">Maytag</a> and <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/amana-not-cooling/">Amana</a>. If it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/refrigerator-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
